--TEST--
`Core\Uri::encodeComponent`'s answer is defined by the parser rather than by its own decoder: interpolated into a path segment, every one of the 128 ASCII bytes survives `Core\Uri::parse` and reads back out of `$uri->path` as itself, while the same bytes written raw leave the path or refuse the parse — spec § 12, RFC 3986 § 3.3
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Every existing case asks this member against its own decoder, which is the
// weaker of the two questions it has to answer: a pair of members could agree
// perfectly with each other and still escape too little to be safe. The claim
// the module doc actually makes — "a segment holding a `/` cannot climb out of
// its position in the path, and a value holding an `&` cannot open a second
// query pair" — is a claim about the *parser*, so it is asserted against the
// parser, over the whole sweep and by counting.

int $survived = 0;
int $readBack = 0;
int $rawStayed = 0;
int $rawMoved = 0;
int $rawRefused = 0;
int $b = 0;
for (; $b <= 127; $b = $b + 1) {
    string $c = Core\Str::fromCodePoint($b as uint);

    // The encoded form. `x` in front of the byte gives the segment content that
    // is not itself in question, so a byte that vanished would show as a
    // shorter path rather than as an empty one.
    string $encoded = "http://h/x" . Core\Uri::encodeComponent($c);
    try {
        var $safe = Core\Uri::parse($encoded);
        $survived = $survived + 1;
        // `decodeComponent` leaves the `/` and the `x` alone, so decoding the
        // whole path is the same question as decoding the segment and needs no
        // member to cut one out.
        if (Core\Uri::decodeComponent($safe->path()) == "/x" . $c) {
            $readBack = $readBack + 1;
        }
    } catch (Throwable $encodedRefused) {
        echo "encoded byte ", $b, " refused\n";
    }

    // The same byte written raw. This half is not a claim about what the parser
    // should do — it is the measurement that says the encoding is doing work:
    // a `?` opens a query, a `#` a fragment, and several bytes the grammar does
    // not admit at all refuse outright.
    string $rawText = "http://h/x" . $c;
    try {
        var $raw = Core\Uri::parse($rawText);
        if ($raw->path() == "/x" . $c) {
            $rawStayed = $rawStayed + 1;
        } else {
            $rawMoved = $rawMoved + 1;
        }
    } catch (Throwable $rawRejected) {
        $rawRefused = $rawRefused + 1;
    }
}
echo "bytes 128, encoded parse ", $survived, ", encoded reads back ", $readBack, "\n";
echo "raw stayed in the path ", $rawStayed, ", raw moved to another component ", $rawMoved,
     ", raw refused the parse ", $rawRefused, "\n";

// The three bytes the sentence in the module doc names, each shown doing the
// thing it is escaped to prevent and then not doing it. A raw `/` adds a
// segment, a raw `?` moves the rest into the query, and a raw `#` into the
// fragment; escaped, all three are one segment's content.
array<string> $climbers = ["/", "?", "#"];
foreach ($climbers as string $climber) {
    var $loose = Core\Uri::parse("http://h/a" . $climber . "b");
    var $tight = Core\Uri::parse("http://h/a" . Core\Uri::encodeComponent($climber) . "b");
    echo "[", $climber, "] raw path [", $loose->path(), "] tight path [", $tight->path(),
         "] decoded [", Core\Uri::decodeComponent($tight->path()), "]\n";
}

// And the query side of the same sentence, which is the other member's job but
// the same property: a value holding an `&` and an `=` is one pair, not three,
// because `buildQuery` writes it through `encodeFormValue`.
var $built = Core\Uri::buildQuery(["q" => "a&b=c"]);
echo "[", $built, "] ", Core\Arr::count(Core\Uri::parseQuery($built)) as int, " pair\n";
--EXPECT--
bytes 128, encoded parse 128, encoded reads back 128
raw stayed in the path 80, raw moved to another component 2, raw refused the parse 46
[/] raw path [/a/b] tight path [/a%2Fb] decoded [/a/b]
[?] raw path [/a] tight path [/a%3Fb] decoded [/a?b]
[#] raw path [/a] tight path [/a%23b] decoded [/a#b]
[q=a%26b%3Dc] 1 pair
